    "wall_seconds": 5.700335205998272,
    "content": "\n\nTo a compiler engineer, speculative decoding is best understood not as a change to the model's logic, but as a **speculative execution** strategy applied to the inference loop, analogous to out-of-order execution or branch prediction in a CPU.\n\nThe core problem it solves is that Large Language Model (LLM) inference is **memory-bandwidth bound**, not compute-bound. Generating a single token requires loading the entire model's weights (potentially tens of gigabytes) from HBM (High Bandwidth Memory) into the GPU's SRAM/registers. Whether you generate one token or a batch of 100 tokens, the cost of moving that data is roughly the same. Therefore, the arithmetic intensity (FLOPs per byte moved) is low, and the GPU's massive compute units sit idle while waiting for memory.\n\n### The Mechanism: The \"Draft\" and the \"Verify\"\n\nSpeculative decoding introduces a two-stage pipeline:\n\n1.  **The Draft Model (The \"Speculator\"):**\n    You deploy a much smaller, faster model (e.g., a 7B parameter model) alongside the large target model (e.g., a 70B parameter model). The draft model generates $k$ tokens sequentially",
