    "wall_seconds": 5.720253295003204,
    "content": "\n\nBelow is a compact but complete treatment: the failure modes you are likely hitting, and a shutdown protocol that is safe under the usual memory model (C++/Java/Go semantics). I\u2019ll assume a classic MPMC queue with a head/tail index, a fixed ring buffer, and a \"closed\" flag.\n\nLikely races that cause lost work\n\n1) Close-then-drain race (most common)\n- Producer:\n  - Checks closed == false\n  - Enqueues item\n- Consumer:\n  - Sets closed = true\n  - Sees queue empty\n  - Exits\n- Result: the item enqueued after the check is never consumed.\n- Root cause: \"closed\" and \"queue empty\" are not observed atomically; there is no happens-before between the producer\u2019s enqueue and the consumer\u2019s final empty check.\n\n2) Consumer exits while a producer is mid-enqueue\n- Consumer sees tail == head (empty), returns.\n- Producer is between reserving a slot and publishing the item (or between publishing and updating tail).\n- Result: item is in the buffer but no one will ever read it.\n\n3) Producer blocks on full queue; consumer closes and exits\n- Producer",
